* Channel subsystem test 3 -- I/O INTERRUPTION, not polling.
*
* Tests 5 and 6 both polled TSCH.  CP does not poll: DMKIOS starts an
* operation and returns to the dispatcher, and DMKIOT runs when the machine
* takes an I/O interruption.  So until an interruption has been taken and a
* subchannel identified from lowcore, the I/O route is unproven in the way
* that matters for CP.
*
* This starts a write, loads an ENABLED WAIT PSW, and expects the machine
* to wake up in a handler at X'78'.
*
* THE THIRD SILENT GATE, AND THE WORST OF THE THREE
* -------------------------------------------------
* Two have already cost time here: CR0's translation format, checked before
* any DAT table is read, and PMCW5_E, without which SSCH returns cc=3 with
* no message.  This one is worse, because the failure is SILENCE.
*
*     /* Isolate the interruption subclass */
*     i = ((dev->pmcw.flag4 & PMCW4_ISC) >> 3);
*     /* Test interruption subclass mask bit in CR6 */
*     if ((regs->CR_L(6) & (0x80000000 >> i)) == 0)
*         return 0;               /* interrupt NOT enabled */
*
* CR6 is the I/O-interruption subclass mask.  A subchannel's ISC defaults
* to 0, so CR6 bit 0 -- X'80000000' -- must be on or the interruption is
* never presented.  Not deferred: never presented.
*
* THIS HAS BEEN MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED.  Running this exact image with CR6
* forced to zero (r 20D8=00000000) gives:
*
*   - the console line STILL PRINTS.  The CCW ran, the device did its work,
*     the I/O genuinely succeeded.
*   - no PSW, no wait-state message, no code.  The CPU sits in its enabled
*     wait until the emulator is killed.
*
* So the trap is not merely silent, it LOOKS LIKE SUCCESS: a developer sees
* the expected output on the console and concludes the I/O path works,
* while the operating system is hung.  A converted CP that gets DMKIOS
* perfectly right and forgets CR6 will IPL, write its initialisation
* message, and stop, with nothing in any log to say why.
*
* HOW TO READ THE RESULT
*   006008   pass.  An interruption was taken, lowcore X'B8' held the
*            expected subsystem id, TSCH returned CE|DE.
*   SILENCE  the interruption was never presented.  Suspect CR6 first, then
*            whether the device attached at all.  Tell them apart at the
*            panel: `cr` shows CR6, `devlist` shows the device.  Note the
*            console message is NOT evidence either way -- see above.
*   000E11   handler entered but lowcore X'B8' was not the expected
*            subsystem id.
*   000E12   handler entered but TSCH gave cc/=0, though status must be
*            pending by definition at that point.
*   000E08   device status not CE|DE.    000E09  subchannel status set.
*   000E01   STSCH cc/=0.  000E02  MSCH cc/=0.  000E03  SSCH cc/=0.
*   000DED   program check -- read the old PSW at X'150'.
*
* KEY BYTES
*   HANDLER at 00203A   the I/O new PSW at X'78' points here
*   IONPSW  at 0020D0 = 000800000000203A   running, I/O masked off
*   WAITPSW at 0020C8 = 020A000000006FFF   byte 0 = 02, I/O ENABLED, wait
*   CR6VAL  at 0020D8 = 80000000           poke 0 here to see the hang
*
* An interruption stores the old PSW and loads the new one; it does not
* touch the general registers.  So R15 is still the base established at
* START and the handler needs no prologue.
*
* WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVE.  One interruption, one subchannel, one CCW.  Not
* an interruption arriving while another is pending, not the ISC mechanism
* with more than one class in play, and not DMKIOT's queue walk.  Those
* need more than one device, which this deliberately minimal config does
* not have.
*
* Built by build/ioint1.py.  Same device, config and MSCH-before-SSCH
* prerequisite as tests 5 and 6 -- see those files for why.
*
